---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { PEERS } from "../data/landscape.js";
---

<BaseLayout>
  <header class="site-header">
    <span class="site-meta">field notes · v0</span>
    <a href="https://github.com/Angelguirao/meta-harness">GitHub</a>
  </header>

  <main id="main">
    <h1 class="brand">meta-harness</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Notes on whatever sits above a coding agent — written while building LifeOS,
      before the industry settles what the word means.
    </p>

    <ul class="toc" aria-label="Sections">
      <li><a href="#claim">Claim</a></li>
      <li><a href="#harness">Harness</a></li>
      <li><a href="#above">Above</a></li>
      <li><a href="#lifeos">LifeOS</a></li>
      <li><a href="#field">Field</a></li>
      <li><a href="#open">Open</a></li>
    </ul>

    <article>
      <section id="claim">
        <h2>What this is</h2>
        <p>
          This is not a product page and not a clone of anyone else’s taxonomy.
          It is a public notebook: problems we keep hitting when agents are the
          workers, guesses about the missing layer, and a short map of peers who
          are touching adjacent pain.
        </p>
        <p>
          If the word <strong>meta-harness</strong> ends up meaning something
          narrower than what LifeOS needs, we will rename. The research is the
          point.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="harness">
        <h2>A harness is the employee</h2>
        <p>
          Claude Code, Codex, Deep Agents, OpenClaw — pick your favorite. A
          harness is the loop that holds tools, a filesystem, a sandbox, and a
          session. You point it at a folder. It reads, edits, tests, opens a PR.
        </p>
        <p>
          That job matters. It is also incomplete for how work actually shows up:
        </p>
        <p>
          The change spans three repos, but the agent only sees one checkout. The
          last session already ruled out an approach — that reasoning died when
          the chat closed. A teammate’s agent burned two hours on the same dead
          end yesterday, invisibly. Policy is a paragraph in
          <code>AGENTS.md</code> that the model sometimes obeys.
        </p>
        <p>
          Git remembers the diff. The session remembered why. The organization
          remembers neither in a form the next agent can use.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="above">
        <h2>Something has to sit above</h2>
        <p>
          Call it a meta-harness, a control plane, a company OS, a synthetic
          monorepo — the label is contested. The job is clearer:
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Continuity</strong> across sessions and people.
          <strong>Scope</strong> across repositories and systems of record.
          <strong>Constraint</strong> that is enforced in code, not hoped for in
          prompts. Optionally a <strong>world model</strong> — durable meaning
          that is not chat transcript.
        </p>
        <p>
          Some products stop at wrap-the-coding-agent. Some want to be the place
          you hire and budget a whole team of agents. Both are answering pressure
          from the same broken defaults. We refuse to pretend they are the same
          object until shipping forces a choice.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="lifeos">
        <h2>What we are actually building</h2>
        <p>
          <a class="in-body" href="https://github.com/Angelguirao/personal-ai-os"
            >LifeOS</a
          > is a personal cognitive OS: folio and ledger as source of truth,
          PersonalOS as the phase walker (observe → plan → execute → validate →
          learn), Fly-hosted Deep Agents as muscle. Agents are workers. They do
          not own the plan pointer.
        </p>
        <p class="stack">
          world model (folio · ledger · trackers)<br />
          → intelligence (PersonalOS OPEVL)<br />
          → execution (Fly Deep Agents · optional Claude/Codex)
        </p>
        <p>
          That stack already behaves like a control plane with harness muscle. It
          only partially behaves like the thin “meta” wraps (session handoff,
          cross-repo orchestration, OS sandbox). The open question is which gap
          is the product — and whether one name should cover both.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="field">
        <h2>Who else is in the field</h2>
        <p>
          Annotated, not ranked. Read them as bets on different slices of the
          pressure above — not as a feature matrix we intend to match.
        </p>
        <ul class="peers">
          {
            PEERS.map((p) => (
              <li>
                <div>
                  {p.url ? (
                    <a href={p.url} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">
                      {p.name}
                    </a>
                  ) : (
                    <span>{p.name}</span>
                  )}
                  <div class="layer">{p.layer}</div>
                </div>
                <p>{p.note}</p>
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section id="open">
        <h2>Still open</h2>
        <ol class="questions">
          <li>
            Is the durable asset the wrap around coding agents, or the world
            model + walker that happens to dispatch them?
          </li>
          <li>
            What must survive a crashed harness for the layer above to have
            earned its keep?
          </li>
          <li>
            For LifeOS, which missing surface forces a real placement call first:
            session handoff, policy sandbox, or cross-repo work?
          </li>
          <li>
            When — if ever — does this stop being a private OS and become
            something other operators should run?
          </li>
        </ol>
      </section>
    </article>
  </main>

  <footer class="site-footer">
    <p>
      Built beside LifeOS. Peers including{" "}
      <a href="https://metaharness.tools/#top">metaharness.tools</a> are
      citations. MIT.
    </p>
  </footer>
</BaseLayout>
